## ===========================================================================
## THE AERIAL RUNWAY — the two-turn slide and the hidden landing
##
## The slide down the anchor cable (`story.ni:3700-3830`, the source's own
## "Aerial Runway" chapter), reshaped by the change document's escape-disguise
## amendments (2026-08-23): the slide takes TWO turns, and the mid-slide text
## reveals the open spot behind the fruit stall — let go there and Jack drops
## in unseen; ride it out and she lands in front of the stall with Gentry's
## knocking-over-shoppers ending, seen.
##
## The machine, in turns:
##
##   1. `slide down cables` (Top of the Post, cloak required) moves Jack to
##      On the Wire and starts the cables' one-turn `sliding` clock. The
##      turn's text is Gentry's first half; the room's own description is the
##      mid-slide reveal.
##   2. `let go` on the mid-slide turn drops her into Behind Fruit Stall —
##      the ONLY way in (change document: "only surfaces from that landing").
##      Any other command lets the clock expire: the slide completes, Gentry's
##      landing block runs unchanged, and she is standing in the Fruit Stall.
##
## Not built yet, by design — these land with the chase + dress increment
## (TE20, the satchel, the boots): the capture on the ride-out landing, the
## one-turn change window behind the stall, the one-turn return window, the
## boots identification at the Fruit Stall, and the mercenaries moving to the
## stall when she lingers. Until then the sweep model alone reads her moves.
##
## Also held: the other three cables' slides (the source's landing table —
## southeast: Hat Stall, southwest: Exotic Gems, northwest: Northwest
## Junction) and the `hang <garment> on cable` entry point with the other
## slide-worthy garments (belt, rope, reins, harness — none exist yet). One
## cable path is the designed one; the rest arrive with the chase.
##
## Structural choices for David to rename or redirect at will: the mid-slide
## room's name ("On the Wire") and Behind Fruit Stall's return exit direction
## (southwest). Every finished line is Gentry's; the marked PLACEHOLDER texts
## are David's to write.
##
## Known seam: `move the player to <room>` narrates nothing, so a landing
## prints only its authored line, not the destination's description (`look`
## still answers). The reveal is therefore emitted as its own phrase on the
## slide turn. Whether the landings should also re-describe is polish for the
## change-window increment.
## ===========================================================================

define timer sliding for the cables
end timer

## The ride-out clock expires after exactly one mid-slide command — Gentry's
## landing block (`story.ni:3822`) runs unchanged; the change document's
## caught-on-landing consequence waits on the chase increment.

create the cables
  aka cable, anchor cables, anchor cable, wires, wire, braided wire
  scenery, plural
  in the Top of the Post
  slideable

  There are four cables, each made of thick, braided wire. They stretch
  southwest, southeast, northeast, and northwest to the four corners of the
  market.

  when sliding expires, while the player is in On the Wire
    phrase slide-ride-out
    move the old gray cloak to the Fruit Stall
    move the player to the Fruit Stall
  end when

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE SLIDE — first turn
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define action sliding
  grammar
    slide the target
    slide down the target
    slide along the target

define trait slideable
  on sliding it
    the player must have the old gray cloak: slide-barehanded
    phrase slide-first-half
    move the player to On the Wire
    phrase on-the-wire-reveal
    start the cables' sliding
  end on
end trait

## The room's description body and the `on-the-wire-reveal` phrase are the
## SAME text, kept in both places: the phrase prints the reveal at the end of
## the slide turn (a `move the player` narrates nothing on its own), and the
## body answers `look` mid-slide. When David writes the line, it goes in both.

create On the Wire
  a room

  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. The mid-slide reveal: halfway down the wire,
  the description shows the open spot behind the fruit stall — let go now and
  that is where she drops; hold on and the slide carries her the rest of the
  way in front of the stall.)

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## LETTING GO — the hidden landing
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define action letting-go
  grammar
    let go
  refuse when the player is not in On the Wire: let-go-nothing
  phrase let-go-drop
  move the old gray cloak to Behind Fruit Stall
  move the player to Behind Fruit Stall
  reset the cables' sliding

create Behind Fruit Stall
  a room
  aka behind the fruit stall
  southwest to the Fruit Stall

  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. The open spot behind the fruit stall: cramped,
  unseen from the market, the one private place to change. Its only exit
  returns to the Fruit Stall.)

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE LINES — Gentry's, split across the two turns
##
## The source's single slide block (`story.ni:3822`) splits at the wind
## sentence: the wrap-and-kick-off half is turn one, the ground-rushes-up
## half is the ride-out. The heartbeat sentences are the source's own
## `[first time]` insert, carried as the first-time arm.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define phrase slide-first-half, first-time
  You wrap one end of your cloak around your hand, then squat down and sling
  the other end around the anchor cable. You give it a good, hard yank to
  test your grip. You take a deep breath. You try to convince your heartbeat
  to slow down, or at least continue its dreadful hammering in some part of
  your body other than your throat. You fail. Then, finally, you swing down
  under the wire and kick off into space.

  The wind is exhilarating as you zip down the line.
or
  You wrap one end of your cloak around your hand, then squat down and sling
  the other end around the anchor cable. You give it a good, hard yank to
  test your grip. Then you swing down under the wire and kick off into space.

  The wind is exhilarating as you zip down the line.
end phrase

define phrase slide-ride-out
  The ground rushes up at you with terrifying speed. Your cloak whizzes hotly
  against the rough wire, and you vaguely smell smoke, but your impromptu
  harness holds. Suddenly your feet hit dirt. You let go of the cloak and
  tumble to the hard ground, knocking over a couple of shoppers before you
  roll to a stop. The next moment you are back on your feet, bruised and
  breathless.
end phrase

define phrase slide-barehanded
  The cable is too steep for you to climb down, and the rough wire would tear
  up your hands. But if you had something you could loop over the cable, you
  might be able to slide down while hanging underneath.
end phrase

define phrase on-the-wire-reveal
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. The mid-slide reveal: halfway down the wire,
  the description shows the open spot behind the fruit stall — let go now and
  that is where she drops; hold on and the slide carries her the rest of the
  way in front of the stall.)
end phrase

define phrase let-go-drop
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. The drop: she lets go mid-slide and lands in
  the open spot behind the fruit stall, unseen.)
end phrase

define phrase let-go-nothing
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. `let go` anywhere but mid-slide.)
end phrase
